**Yes.** In Washington, a landlord has to give you a signed move-in condition checklist before collecting any security deposit.

**What the rule says:**
- **The checklist is required to collect a deposit.** It has to describe the condition of the unit, including fixtures, equipment, appliances and furnishings. This comes from RCW 59.18.260, which also requires a written rental agreement for a deposit.
- **Damage not on the checklist can't be taken out of your deposit.** The landlord can only deduct for things the checklist documented.
- **Normal wear and tear can't be deducted either.** Neither can replacing a whole item when only part of it is damaged.

**Other Washington deposit rules:**
- **No statewide cap** on the deposit amount, though some cities set their own limits.
- **Return deadline:** After you move out, the landlord has 30 days to return your deposit or send a written statement of what they kept, with documentation. This was 21 days before July 2023.
- **Penalties:** If the landlord misses the deadline, they can lose the right to keep any of the deposit. If they refuse to return it on purpose, a court can award up to twice the deposit. Whoever wins the case gets their attorney's fees paid.

**What to do now:**
1. If you paid a deposit and never signed a checklist, keep a written record of that. It could matter if the landlord tries to make deductions later.
2. If you did get a checklist, make sure it lists every existing problem before you sign it. Keep a copy.
3. Either way, take dated photos or video of the unit now.

**Source:** [PropInspect – Washington Security Deposit Laws](https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/washington), last reviewed August 27, 2026. It's based on Washington Revised Code §§ 59.18.260–.280. You can read the checklist rule in the official statute, [RCW 59.18.260](https://apps.leg.wa.gov/rcw/default.aspx?cite=59.18.260).

